00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.489
So, Mark, thank you for
agreeing to kick this off.

00:03:42.489 --> 00:03:43.690
We're all very much
looking forward

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.470
to what you have to say.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:46.470
Thanks, again.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.559
Dr. Mark Musen: And thank you,
I'm real grateful.

00:03:49.559 --> 00:03:51.719
This is rather an
awesome responsibility

00:03:51.719 --> 00:03:53.769
to give the first
lecture in this series.

00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:55.629
And I'm actually
very excited to do,

00:03:55.629 --> 00:03:57.609
because I think
the lectures that

00:03:57.609 --> 00:04:00.290
will follow me will
be even, perhaps,

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:02.549
more interesting than
this general overview.

00:04:02.549 --> 00:04:04.719
But I think it's very
important to sort of frame

00:04:04.719 --> 00:04:06.599
what we'll be doing
over the next few weeks,

00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:09.579
and to give you a general
introduction to data science,

00:04:09.579 --> 00:04:13.150
and where we are heading, and
where the field is heading.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:14.980
So as you know, this
is the first lecture

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:16.949
of a whole series
that's been put together

00:04:16.949 --> 00:04:20.370
by the BD2K Training
Coordinating Center,

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:23.120
with help from the Centers
Coordinating Center,

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.470
with the NIH staff,
and with a little bit of help

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.070
from me.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:28.940
And we're very,
very excited to be

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:32.890
able to give you a whole series
of lectures in data science

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:36.130
that will, obviously, be
very exciting this quarter,

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.660
and then will continue
through the rest of the year.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:40.150
What I want to do
this morning is

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:42.190
to foreshadow a
lot of the things

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:44.170
that you'll hear about
from the other speakers,

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:47.010
and to give you a sense for
why there's so much excitement

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:51.190
about data science, and why
this field is so important.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:55.040
And it, clearly, is important,
because the world is changing.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.880
We think in terms of data
in ways which we probably

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:58.880
never did before.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.420
We used to think of
data as sort of the side

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:02.940
effects of scientific research.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:07.130
And now, we recognize that
data are first class entities

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:10.630
of themselves that also
require careful consideration,

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:12.440
careful thought,
careful planning.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.630
And what I want
to do this morning

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:17.660
is to give you a sense for
what some of that planning

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:19.941
needs to be, in order
to get the most out

00:05:19.941 --> 00:05:23.220
of the kinds of data that
are acquired as consequence

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.910
of scientific investigation.

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:28.280
A lot of stuff is
floating around the web

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:31.990
about how the world is
changing because of data.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:34.530
There was a recent
piece on PBS that

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:37.150
made claims such as,
all the data processing

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:40.520
we did in the last two years
is more than all the data

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:43.420
processing we did in
the last 3,000 years.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:46.610
Or we are now being exposed
to as much information

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:49.990
in a single day as our 15th
century ancestors were exposed

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:52.520
to in their entire lifetime.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:53.650
And ever two days,
the human race

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:56.830
is now generating
as much data as were

00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:59.640
generated from the dawn of
humanity through the year 2003.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:04.370
These are, obviously,
breathtaking sentences.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:06.680
Frankly, no one
quite knows where

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:09.020
are the data that support
these particular sentences.

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:13.120
But it gives you a sense for
why the world is recognizing

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.900
that the onslaught slot
of information around us

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.280
clearly is changing the
way we live qualitatively.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:21.220
And biomedicine--
we have the onset

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:24.140
of the [INAUDIBLE] revolution,
which has led to more data

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.210
than anyone could ever imagine.

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:28.950
In clinical medicine, we had
the advent of electronic health

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.340
records, whose data
are now being mined

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:35.530
in all kinds of ways, and where
we recognize that we may not

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:37.610
always have the skills
that we need in order

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:39.420
to appreciate the
kinds of information

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:43.840
there are coming around
us in biomedicine.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:47.090
The term big data, of course,
is what's on everyone's lips.

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:49.050
And at the same
time, it's a word

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:51.080
that has a lot of overload.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:54.950
It has a lot of
associations made with it.

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:58.710
I'm not going to try to
define big data intentionally.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:00.580
I think that's
kind of hard to do.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:02.270
But it's very easy
to describe it

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:03.980
in terms of its characteristics.

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:05.300
And the three that
are often mentioned

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:10.560
are the idea of data volume--
just a lot of data, velocity,

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:14.150
the way in which data
comes at us very quickly,

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:16.400
and variety, the
heterogeneity in the data

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:17.760
that we're acquiring.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.930
And to that, people
often had the idea

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:23.460
that there's a
question of veracity.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:24.710
We get lots of data.

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:27.060
We don't always know the
truth value of the data

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:29.130
that we've received.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:32.290
And volume is not what
I meant to say here.

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:36.711
But, yes, I will remember
the fifth V later.

00:07:36.711 --> 00:07:43.490
This is just a slide from IBM
trying to sort of summarize

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:47.520
what they believe being the way
in which they view big data.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.310
And I think it's important
not to necessarily

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:54.310
look at all of the examples
that they give on their slide,

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:57.330
but to make it clear how much
the big data revolution is

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:59.470
affecting not only what
we do in science and also

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:00.750
what's happening in industry.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:03.750
And we see that throughout
society, there's

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:09.471
this increasing desire to be
able to identify where data are

00:08:09.471 --> 00:08:13.120
coming from, to deal with
data, and to be able to prepare

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.501
for the fact that, in the future
the amount of data that we deal

00:08:15.501 --> 00:08:20.270
with will even be greater.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:22.840
And although it's easy
to think about big data

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:25.130
and emphasize the
word big, and think

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:28.490
about quantitative
differences, I

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:31.010
think what makes things
important in data science

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:35.099
is the recognition that
data-- because of the way they

00:08:35.099 --> 00:08:38.159
are changing our world-- are
affecting things qualitatively.

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:42.280
Suddenly, we're in a situation
where our servers no longer

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:44.000
can store all the
data that we used

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:45.510
to store in a single place.

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:48.130
And we have to think
about new solutions, both

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:51.000
in terms of hardware and
the kinds of data systems

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.330
that those hardware support.

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:54.910
We have to think
about new algorithms,

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:57.980
because we suddenly can't
process data in one sitting.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:02.860
We suddenly have to recognize
that, because of the volume,

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:06.120
and velocity, and everything
else about the data,

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:09.010
we need to have algorithms
that can keep up with them.

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:12.430
And probably most important,
although not something

00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:15.970
that gets a lot of press, is
that we're moving into an era

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:18.110
where we can't get our
arms around the data.

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:19.279
We can't look at the data.

00:09:19.279 --> 00:09:20.560
We can't really
understand, intuitively,

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:22.890
what the data all mean.

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:25.210
And so there's a need to be
able to deal with the fact

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:27.120
that our cognitive
ability is strained,

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:30.210
because without
the ability to deal

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:33.690
with the visualization of
data, and to get an intuitive

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:35.300
understanding of
data, we often have

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:37.660
to go purely on the
basis of our algorithms,

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:39.990
which, obviously,
limits our ability

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:46.930
to develop intuition about
the work that we're doing.

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:48.310
This is a side
that was generated

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:51.790
by Forrester Research
trying to identify what

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:55.090
are all the various
technologies that are required

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:56.630
to manage the data revolution?

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:59.010
I'm not going to go through
this slide in detail,

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:01.130
because, frankly, a lot of
the stuff that's on the slide

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:02.980
is going to be
handled in the talks

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:05.880
that you're going to be hearing
in this series coming up.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:07.160
But I think what's
important to emphasize

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:11.500
from this slide is the fact
that Forrester believes

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:15.040
we've had significant success
in a lot of the infrastructure

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:16.400
needed to manage big data.

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:20.370
And what's interesting is that
all of these big successes

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:22.790
happen very rapidly.

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:24.720
And the time to reach
the next phase, whatever

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:26.839
that next phase might be
in each one of these areas,

00:10:26.839 --> 00:10:28.120
is not that long.

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:31.380
And so as far as the
infrastructure is concerned,

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:33.780
Forrester would argue that
we're making progress.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:37.120
And, certainly, when it
comes to biomedicine,

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:39.760
NIH recognized a couple
years ago the importance

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:42.330
of addressing the big
data challenges that

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:43.960
are facing us in biomedicine.

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:48.640
And as you know, the creation
of the Big Data To Knowledge

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:50.450
Initiative, and
this seminar series

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:52.470
which is part of
that initiative,

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:55.810
has been a major undertaking
on the part of the NIH

00:10:55.810 --> 00:11:00.560
to be able to move biology and
medicine into the next era.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:01.960
And so I think there's
a lot of excitement.

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:03.240
There's a lot of hype.

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:04.820
But there's also
a lot of progress

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:06.290
that's being made in this area.

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:07.750
And that's, obviously,
the important stuff

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:12.180
that we'll be talking
about in this seminar.

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:15.220
What I want to do this morning
is to begin with a story.

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:19.140
And my story, basically, is
that of a colleague of mine,

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:22.910
Purvesh Khatri, who is a
faculty member at Stanford,

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:25.290
and who describes himself
as a data parasite.

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.089
And I think it's
important to give you

00:11:27.089 --> 00:11:29.840
a sense for the kind of
work that Purvesh does,

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:33.210
because I think it motivates a
lot of our concerns about data

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:34.940
and the data life
cycle, which I'll be

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:37.800
getting to later in the hour.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:41.130
So Purvesh is mainly concerned
about functional genomics data.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:44.380
He knows that out there are
all kinds of microarray data

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:47.430
that can be very useful in
understanding how genes get

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:49.180
turned and turned
off in response

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:52.650
to various biological signals.

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:55.720
And he knows that
all of the data that

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:58.900
correspond to those
kinds of experiments that

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:01.190
are at least performed
in the United States

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:05.270
are available to the NCBI's
Gene Expression Omnibus, or GEO.

00:12:05.270 --> 00:12:09.160
Go out to GEO, and in
this large database,

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:10.800
you will be able
to find information

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:13.380
about basically every
microarray experiment that's

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:15.180
ever been done.

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:18.660
And Purvesh goes to GEO
with the express desire

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:22.529
to understand what he can
learn from the data that

00:12:22.529 --> 00:12:24.720
had been collected by
other investigators

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:28.370
and deposited in the
GEO data repository.

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:30.589
So Khatri, basically,
is building his career

00:12:30.589 --> 00:12:33.250
about studying
other people's data.

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:35.370
And, basically,
what that means is

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:37.880
looking at the publicly
available data that

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:40.150
are out there on
the web in order

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:41.970
to understand what are
the different kinds

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.300
of experimental conditions that
investigators are considering,

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:48.710
what kind of real world
heterogeneity might enter

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:50.970
into their data
sets, and recognizing

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:52.190
that he can do all
this without having

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:53.990
to perform his own experiments.

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:56.430
So there's really no
experimental cost, at least

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:58.580
in terms of the initial
data collection.

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:01.450
He can look at human samples.

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:03.980
And he doesn't have to
worry about IRB approval,

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:05.470
because that's
already been done.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:08.560
And what Khatri has done is
to create a pipeline that

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:11.750
allows him to search
for datasets in GEO,

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:14.880
to look for genomic
signals, to confirm

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:18.290
those signals in validation data
sets, and then to confirm what

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:23.060
he sees-- rather to
[INAUDIBLE] in a test set,

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:26.120
and then to confirm those
signals in validation data

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:27.330
sets.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:29.250
And what this
allows him to do is

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:32.339
to see if he can infer
information from existing

00:13:32.339 --> 00:13:35.740
microarray studies that may
not have been considered

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:37.460
by the investigators
who did those studies

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:40.810
in the first place, but
where he can make discoveries

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.990
on the existing data
sets without ever

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:45.560
having to perform an
additional experiment.

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:49.110
And so the outline
of that flow of work

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:51.400
is shown on the right
hand side of the slide.

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:54.910
And he's demonstrated the
value of this approach

00:13:54.910 --> 00:13:56.500
in a wide range of areas.

00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:58.510
One that's gotten a
lot of press lately

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:00.700
is that of diagnosis of sepsis.

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:02.761
So sepsis is,
basically, a response

00:14:02.761 --> 00:14:06.190
to infection in the
body that can be very

00:14:06.190 --> 00:14:07.580
difficult to detect at first.

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:11.330
It can be confused with
lots of other common kinds

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:14.430
of abnormalities that occur
in hospitalized patients.

00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:17.840
Usually, it's response to
infection in the blood.

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:20.570
But there can be lots
of non-infectious causes

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:23.210
of inflammation that can look
a lot like sepsis as well.

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:25.990
And so coming up
with this diagnosis

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:28.300
is, obviously, critical, because
unless you make this diagnosis

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:32.400
and treat the underlying
infection, patients die.

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:35.100
And what Purvesh has
done is to go out

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:37.100
to GEO, the Gene
Expression Omnibus,

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:40.350
looking at functional genomics
data with a clinical question.

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:42.920
Are there changes
in gene expression

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:45.690
that can predict the
advent of sepsis?

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:50.640
And Purvesh has gone out
there, found some sample

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:54.300
data, some validation data
looking at his initial samples.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:58.520
He looked at nine
cohorts with 663 samples,

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:01.780
identified candidate
genes, 82 candidate genes,

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:05.420
that might be suggestive
of the onset of sepsis,

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:07.800
and, ultimately,
validated 11 genes which

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:10.960
showed to be important,
both in his test

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:14.680
set and his validation set.

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:16.420
And when he looks
at those genes,

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:18.800
he shows really
phenomenal ROC curves

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:21.480
that show that these genes
are very, very sensitive,

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:26.029
and get turned on when
sepsis is imminent.

00:15:26.029 --> 00:15:29.580
And he can show an effect,
where with the advent of sepsis,

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:33.120
with each passing day
those genes get turned on

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:35.490
to even greater degrees.

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:37.890
I'm not going to go into the
details of Purvesh's numbers.

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:40.290
But they're just
absolutely exciting,

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:42.450
because this is a way
of trying to identify

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:45.510
a new way of diagnosing
a medical condition

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:49.300
purely on the basis
of other people's data

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:51.990
stored in a public repository.

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:53.610
And Purvesh has
taken this approach,

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:58.010
and applied it to not only the
diagnosis of incipient sepsis,

00:15:58.010 --> 00:16:00.720
but also the diagnosis
of tuberculosis,

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:03.780
and distinguishing active
TB from disease that

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:06.250
is burned out, which is
actually very hard to do

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:09.420
clinically, distinguishing
viral respiratory

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:11.940
infection from bacterial
respiratory infection--

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:15.320
another very important clinical
challenge that usually results

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:19.410
in a lot of over-medication
of patients antibiotics that

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:21.960
can be avoided if one
knows that something is bio

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:22.960
from the beginning.

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:24.620
He's used this same
approach to identify

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:27.670
rejection of organ transplants
and other difficult diagnostic

00:16:27.670 --> 00:16:30.470
problems in clinical medicine.

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:33.220
All of these challenges are
really tough to do clinically,

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:35.890
but can be addressed
easily by looking

00:16:35.890 --> 00:16:38.040
at changes in gene expression.

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:40.850
And the most exciting thing
about this whole adventure

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:44.320
that Purvesh has been
undergoing is that he's never

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:45.780
touched a pipette.

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:47.550
He's never had to kill a mouse.

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:49.899
He's never actually had
to do the experiments that

00:16:49.899 --> 00:16:52.279
lead to those data,
because the people who

00:16:52.279 --> 00:16:55.470
have done the experiments have
been very cognizant of what's

00:16:55.470 --> 00:16:57.680
needed in order
to make their data

00:16:57.680 --> 00:16:59.519
not only valuable
for the experiments

00:16:59.519 --> 00:17:02.930
that they are planning, but
also valuable for the future

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:04.939
of scientific work.

00:17:04.939 --> 00:17:08.540
So you can be a data
parasite like Purvesh Khatri

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:11.160
only if the data that
you might want to find

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:13.740
are in some public
repository, that

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:16.160
are findable through some
sort of a search facility,

00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:19.160
that you can in
some standard format

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:22.980
that you parse and
compute with, and most

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:25.100
important, if the data
themselves are self-describing

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:27.460
so that you know how to
make sense of the data.

00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:29.570
They're not just a bunch
columns of information,

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:32.150
but rather they actually
can describe themselves

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:34.270
to understand what
the columns mean.

00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:36.020
And you can make
sense those columns,

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:39.450
and that the product of
careful planning, organization,

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:40.450
and stewardship.

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:44.220
And they're basically created in
a way where the data themselves

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:47.059
are intended to be reused
and to outlive the experiment

00:17:47.059 --> 00:17:48.059
from which they were collected.

00:17:48.059 --> 00:17:50.179
And I guess that's
the key thing,

00:17:50.179 --> 00:17:51.940
because in the old
school of thought,

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:54.030
we might think of
data as being the side

00:17:54.030 --> 00:17:55.320
effects of our experiment.

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:56.500
We publish our paper.

00:17:56.500 --> 00:18:00.550
And the paper that we publish
is a result of our research.

00:18:00.550 --> 00:18:03.540
And I think, currently,
the tide is shifting.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:05.620
And we're thinking about
the data themselves

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:07.130
as being the output
of our investigation.

00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:13.900
And we're thinking about data
as having a lifecycle that

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:15.820
starts with the idea
of planning what we're

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:19.230
going to do with the
data, collecting the data,

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:22.100
performing quality
assurance on the data,

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:24.860
describing those data so that
we understand what the data are

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:27.030
about, preserving
them in an archive,

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:29.480
making sure that it's
possible for other people

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:31.670
to discover those
data, to integrate

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:34.850
those data with other data
that they might have available,

00:18:34.850 --> 00:18:36.610
to perform new kinds
of analyses that

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:39.170
might lead to new insights
that might cause us to do

00:18:39.170 --> 00:18:41.790
new kinds of experiments, which
themselves will require us

00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:43.850
to begin this life cycle,
to plan experiments,

00:18:43.850 --> 00:18:49.070
to collect data, and to
perform QA, and so on.

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:51.390
We've moved into a
world, basically,

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:55.790
where the experiment begins
not with the definition

00:18:55.790 --> 00:18:58.670
of the protocol that we're
going to follow, as much

00:18:58.670 --> 00:19:02.280
as the definition of the
plan for data management

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:05.290
that will be necessary in
order to perform the protocol,

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:07.410
and make sure that
the data themselves

00:19:07.410 --> 00:19:10.460
outlive the experiment
that we're planning.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:12.820
And so what I want
to do is allow

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:14.910
us to follow this
data life cycle

00:19:14.910 --> 00:19:18.840
and get a sense of what these
various steps are, emphasizing

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:20.760
that each one of
these steps is going

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:22.080
to be the subject
of a talk you'll

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:26.850
hear about later in
this colloquium series.

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:32.110
Let me start by saying
that one begins with plans.

00:19:32.110 --> 00:19:39.410
And the data management plans
that guide our work, basically,

00:19:39.410 --> 00:19:42.360
are required now by almost
every finding agency, because

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:43.670
of executive orders.

00:19:43.670 --> 00:19:46.910
At the NIH, if you
have a project that

00:19:46.910 --> 00:19:50.650
involves more than $500,000
of direct costs every year,

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:52.610
you must have a data
management plan.

00:19:52.610 --> 00:19:54.980
If you deal with geonomic
or sequence data,

00:19:54.980 --> 00:19:56.850
then you must have a
data management plan.

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:58.450
And, frankly, if
you're smart, you're

00:19:58.450 --> 00:20:01.120
going to have a data management
plan, because the data

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:03.890
management plan saves lots of
time and effort down the road

00:20:03.890 --> 00:20:05.790
by making sure we know what
we're going to do with the data

00:20:05.790 --> 00:20:07.450
when we get them.

00:20:07.450 --> 00:20:10.040
Most important, they
enhance the durability

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:11.940
and the long-term
value of the data

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:14.040
so that investigators
like Purvesh

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:16.220
can find these data
later, and identify

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:19.419
even new kinds of inferences
that can come from those data.

00:20:19.419 --> 00:20:22.230
As I said, they're
required by sponsors.

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:25.970
Unfortunately, although the
data plans are required,

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:29.980
they're almost never
reviewed as a consequence

00:20:29.980 --> 00:20:31.500
to peer review, at
least at this point.

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:32.500
That may change.

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:33.600
But right now,
the requirement is

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:36.780
that the data plan exists--
not necessarily that it satisfy

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:40.480
the criteria of peer review.

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:41.830
But it's there.

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:44.049
And, frankly, in
the best projects,

00:20:44.049 --> 00:20:46.680
the data plan is not
created once at the start,

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:50.080
but rather is revisited and
updated throughout the project

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:52.090
so that we can make
sure that we're dealing

00:20:52.090 --> 00:20:53.450
with data appropriately.

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:57.700
That data plan requires
us to be thinking about,

00:20:57.700 --> 00:21:00.630
what is the project that
we're actually doing?

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:03.790
What are the existing data
that may already be available

00:21:03.790 --> 00:21:07.440
that we want to be able to
consider in our experiments?

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:09.470
What are the new data that
we're going to be generating?

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:11.840
What are the methods
and the instruments

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:12.980
for the data collection?

00:21:12.980 --> 00:21:16.350
If we collect the data,
how do we organize them?

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:19.710
What is the data storage format?

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:20.710
What are the structures?

00:21:20.710 --> 00:21:23.760
What is the mechanism by
which we store the data?

00:21:23.760 --> 00:21:25.080
What permissions
do we need to allow

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:26.290
people to access the data?

00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:28.030
Are we going to backup the data?

00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:29.030
Hopefully.

00:21:29.030 --> 00:21:30.760
How often will we
backup, and how?

00:21:30.760 --> 00:21:33.270
What kind of data scrubbing,
or quality assurance,

00:21:33.270 --> 00:21:34.630
do we anticipate?

00:21:34.630 --> 00:21:37.320
What are the administrative,
legal, and ethical concerns?

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:39.520
Are we dealing with
human subjects data?

00:21:39.520 --> 00:21:41.929
How are we going to
protect human subjects?

00:21:41.929 --> 00:21:44.400
How do we archive
and publish our data?

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:46.030
What are the
responsibilities and duties

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:47.540
of the various project
members who are going

00:21:47.540 --> 00:21:49.350
to be contributing to the data?

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:50.600
And what are the
costs and resources

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:53.640
that we need to do
this experiment?

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:56.480
Lots of stuff-- it's
a little bit daunting.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:22:00.080
The good news is that there
are open source tools that

00:22:00.080 --> 00:22:01.080
are readily available.

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:03.770
There's one called DMP tool
that almost all research

00:22:03.770 --> 00:22:05.960
universities seem to be using.

00:22:05.960 --> 00:22:08.750
And there are lots of
easy ways for, basically,

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:12.210
using templates to create
data management plans,

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:14.870
and to make sure that they're
available with the proposals

00:22:14.870 --> 00:22:18.980
that we submit, even before we
begin to do experimentation.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:22.390
And at the same time, because
these are available and easily

00:22:22.390 --> 00:22:24.380
editable, they allow
us to make sure

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:27.740
that our data
management plans evolve,

00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:30.160
as we recognize
additional complications

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:32.230
as experiments get performed.

00:22:32.230 --> 00:22:35.350
So then we collect the data.

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:37.430
And collecting the
data can be done

00:22:37.430 --> 00:22:40.350
in a gazillion different
ways, because, frankly, there

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:43.370
are a gazillion kinds of data.

00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:46.169
And so our data management
plan provides the guidance

00:22:46.169 --> 00:22:48.970
that allows us to understand
how to deal with the data

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:49.970
that we're collecting.

00:22:49.970 --> 00:22:51.679
Are those data
going to be coming

00:22:51.679 --> 00:22:52.770
from laboratory observations?

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:55.400
Are they going to be coming
directly from instruments?

00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:57.730
Are they coming from
surveys that we may

00:22:57.730 --> 00:22:59.050
administer to human subjects?

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:03.990
Are they coming from
continuous electronic signals?

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:06.590
Are they coming from
discrete values,

00:23:06.590 --> 00:23:08.330
such as microarray chips?

00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:12.170
All kinds of data
are used in biomedicine.

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:14.860
And we have to understand
how to get them in one place

00:23:14.860 --> 00:23:19.270
so that we can begin
to analyze them.

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:23.240
Data may also not be coming
from de novo experimentation.

00:23:23.240 --> 00:23:26.240
But as I showed in the
case of the kind of work

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:27.840
that Purvesh Khatri does.

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:30.210
They may come from
online repositories.

00:23:30.210 --> 00:23:32.190
So our collection
of data may not

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:33.710
come from direct
experimentation,

00:23:33.710 --> 00:23:36.890
but from gathering data from
the publicly available data

00:23:36.890 --> 00:23:39.980
sets that other investigators
have put online.

00:23:39.980 --> 00:23:42.250
And sometimes, if we're
dealing with meta analyses

00:23:42.250 --> 00:23:44.799
or systematic reviews,
we may be getting data

00:23:44.799 --> 00:23:48.530
not from actual data sets,
but from other publications.

00:23:48.530 --> 00:23:51.320
And it's amassing the
publications themselves,

00:23:51.320 --> 00:23:53.460
in order to perform our work.

00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:56.549
Now, on September
30th, we're going

00:23:56.549 --> 00:24:01.799
to have a talk on data curation
by Pascal Gaudet

00:24:01.799 --> 00:24:05.140
On November 4th, we're going
to hear about data warehousing

00:24:05.140 --> 00:24:06.390
from Chaitan Baru.

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:08.299
So be prepared for
all kinds of talk

00:24:08.299 --> 00:24:11.110
coming up that will deal
with these kinds of aspects

00:24:11.110 --> 00:24:14.120
of the data lifecycle.

00:24:14.120 --> 00:24:17.120
Quality assurance is sort of
the next step in that lifecycle.

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:19.950
Sometimes, people call
it data scrubbing.

00:24:19.950 --> 00:24:24.080
And it is actually a
science in and of itself.

00:24:24.080 --> 00:24:27.240
[INAUDIBLE] will give a talk
on this on December 2nd.

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:31.280
We'll talk about how to double
check the values of data that

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:34.679
are edited by hand, how to
identify data that may have

00:24:34.679 --> 00:24:37.230
quality problems that
need to get flagged,

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:39.620
how we can use statistics to
understand where we might have

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:42.510
data outliers, how we can
identify where data might be

00:24:42.510 --> 00:24:45.590
impossible, how we can
identify missing data,

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:48.919
and maybe correct the
values for missing data

00:24:48.919 --> 00:24:53.530
or impute replacements, how
to deal with those outliers.

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:56.840
Basically, there's a whole
science, if you will,

00:24:56.840 --> 00:25:00.049
to dealing with the fact
that our data are usually

00:25:00.049 --> 00:25:01.750
full of errors.

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:04.230
And before we actually make
data publicly available

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:07.289
and apply our own investigative
techniques for those data,

00:25:07.289 --> 00:25:09.200
there are things that
we need to do in order

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:12.560
to make sure that the data are
going to meet our expectations,

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:15.240
and be reliable for our own use.

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:21.710
So we plan our data overall,
our data management overall.

00:25:21.710 --> 00:25:22.770
We collect the data.

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:24.840
We perform quality
assurance and scrubbing.

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:27.330
And then, we're in
a situation where

00:25:27.330 --> 00:25:30.000
we need to describe the data.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:33.669
So we can't just have data
and stick it in a drawer.

00:25:33.669 --> 00:25:36.220
We have to be able to recognize
that we need meta data.

00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:39.180
And as Jason Scott
says, metadata

00:25:39.180 --> 00:25:41.590
is a love note to the future.

00:25:41.590 --> 00:25:44.510
It's our ability to describe
data so that down the road,

00:25:44.510 --> 00:25:46.570
we can figure out what we did.

00:25:46.570 --> 00:25:48.809
And we can make sure
that other people who

00:25:48.809 --> 00:25:52.600
may want to think about data,
or think about our data,

00:25:52.600 --> 00:25:54.460
know what is in
the data, and how

00:25:54.460 --> 00:25:56.299
to make sense of those data.

00:25:56.299 --> 00:25:58.800
So metadata describe lots
and lots of different things.

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:00.240
They describe the
digital content.

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:02.160
So what are the
actual properties

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:05.360
of the files themselves
that store the data?

00:26:05.360 --> 00:26:07.700
They provide a
mechanism for clarifying

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:08.880
who are the investigators?

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:11.980
And who are the stakeholders
who care about this data?

00:26:11.980 --> 00:26:13.590
What is the scientific context?

00:26:13.590 --> 00:26:17.720
So, basically, why was
the experiment done?

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:19.880
What were the data that
are actually collected?

00:26:19.880 --> 00:26:22.919
What were the methods
that were applied?

00:26:22.919 --> 00:26:26.700
What instruments were used to
acquire the data-- not only

00:26:26.700 --> 00:26:28.720
the kind of instruments,
but actually

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:29.809
the physical instruments?

00:26:29.809 --> 00:26:34.480
What was the serial number
of the instrument used?

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:37.600
When and where were
the data collected?

00:26:37.600 --> 00:26:40.210
And, basically, what are
the parameters of the data?

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:41.559
Lots of stuff.

00:26:41.559 --> 00:26:44.410
I should mention that this
list of metadata attributes

00:26:44.410 --> 00:26:47.040
comes from the Data One primer
on data management, which

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:48.040
I think is great.

00:26:48.040 --> 00:26:50.750
I'll give that as a reference
at the end of this talk,

00:26:50.750 --> 00:26:54.950
and also let you know
that on October 21st,

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:56.530
Susanna Sansone
from Oxford is going

00:26:56.530 --> 00:26:58.610
to give a talk on metadata
and metadata standards.

00:26:58.610 --> 00:27:02.440
And I think talk will really
be very, very helpful.

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:05.440
Now, I mentioned
that not only do

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:08.289
we want to understand the
experiment in the metadata.

00:27:08.289 --> 00:27:10.520
We want to understand the
parameters of the data.

00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:11.950
That may seem pretty trivial.

00:27:11.950 --> 00:27:14.750
But, in fact, there's
lots of evidence

00:27:14.750 --> 00:27:16.390
that shows that when
we don't do that,

00:27:16.390 --> 00:27:18.400
we get into really big trouble.

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:21.049
We certainly have good examples
where you have trouble making

00:27:21.049 --> 00:27:22.500
sense of manageable data.

00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:24.130
I think one of
the best anecdotes

00:27:24.130 --> 00:27:28.370
is what happened in 1998
when we sent the Mars Climate

00:27:28.370 --> 00:27:29.440
Orbiter to Mars.

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:31.309
It's a great anecdote.

00:27:31.309 --> 00:27:33.650
It's actually an
unfortunate anecdote,

00:27:33.650 --> 00:27:37.480
because it really was a
$328 million disaster.

00:27:37.480 --> 00:27:40.210
As those of you who may have
remembered this misadventure,

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:44.900
the manufacturers
of the satellite

00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:49.000
did their work correctly, and
followed the specifications,

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:52.919
and managed all their data
using the metric system.

00:27:52.919 --> 00:27:57.020
But when NASA sent data up
to the Mars Climate Orbiter,

00:27:57.020 --> 00:28:00.220
those data were sent
in the English system.

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:02.650
And suddenly getting
data in foot pounds

00:28:02.650 --> 00:28:05.710
was something that really
threw off the satellite.

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:08.500
And it crashed into
the Martian surface.

00:28:08.500 --> 00:28:10.309
And the idea that NASA
would not recognize

00:28:10.309 --> 00:28:13.970
that there needed to be unit
conversion was, obviously,

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:15.909
rather embarrassing,
and probably provides

00:28:15.909 --> 00:28:19.780
one of the best examples of
why not only does are metadata

00:28:19.780 --> 00:28:22.840
need to describe what
experiments we did,

00:28:22.840 --> 00:28:25.460
but also provide the
data-specific information that

00:28:25.460 --> 00:28:27.980
allows us to
understand what it is

00:28:27.980 --> 00:28:32.350
that-- how our data are
actually represented,

00:28:32.350 --> 00:28:36.330
what those various units are.

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:38.080
So the metadata
about the parameters

00:28:38.080 --> 00:28:41.460
will include how each parameter
is measured or produced,

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:44.460
what its units measure
are, the formats used

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:46.570
in the dataset, the
precision, accuracy,

00:28:46.570 --> 00:28:51.950
and uncertainty with which the
data values are represented,

00:28:51.950 --> 00:28:55.419
definitions of any
codes that are used,

00:28:55.419 --> 00:28:58.169
what kinds of quality assurance
was performed on the data,

00:28:58.169 --> 00:29:00.490
known problems that
can limit the data use,

00:29:00.490 --> 00:29:01.900
and how to cite the dataset.

00:29:01.900 --> 00:29:04.190
These are real things,
of course, that we

00:29:04.190 --> 00:29:05.190
would like our metadata to say.

00:29:05.190 --> 00:29:08.620
And I guess one thing that I
should be making very clear

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:13.090
is that we often do--
actually, we usually

00:29:13.090 --> 00:29:15.549
don't have metadata that
describe information

00:29:15.549 --> 00:29:17.659
at any of this level of detail.

00:29:17.659 --> 00:29:21.659
And, of course, one of the goals
of the research in data science

00:29:21.659 --> 00:29:25.280
is to make it easier
and better for us

00:29:25.280 --> 00:29:28.590
to be able to make sure
that our data are described

00:29:28.590 --> 00:29:31.990
at the right level of detail.

00:29:31.990 --> 00:29:33.429
So if we go to the Gene
Expression Omnibus,

00:29:33.429 --> 00:29:37.260
this is the place where we
store all of the microarray data

00:29:37.260 --> 00:29:40.960
that, for example, Purvesh
Khatri accesses, one

00:29:40.960 --> 00:29:43.539
of the things that
is very clear is

00:29:43.539 --> 00:29:47.049
that there is a
template for how data in GEO

00:29:47.049 --> 00:29:49.940
need to be represented
in the database.

00:29:49.940 --> 00:29:53.590
And this isn't just any old
template created by NCBI.

00:29:53.590 --> 00:29:56.370
Basically, the framework
for representing

00:29:56.370 --> 00:30:01.390
gene expression data in GEO
stems from a description that

00:30:01.390 --> 00:30:05.990
is now almost 16 years old
called the minimum information

00:30:05.990 --> 00:30:09.330
standard about a microarray
experiment, or MIAMI.

00:30:09.330 --> 00:30:11.659
And MIAMI basically
represents the work

00:30:11.659 --> 00:30:15.690
of a community of investigators
who got together and recognized

00:30:15.690 --> 00:30:19.810
that people were storing
microarray data online.

00:30:19.810 --> 00:30:21.770
But they were doing it in
a rather ad hoc fashion.

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:25.929
And it was often impossible
to look at the online data,

00:30:25.929 --> 00:30:28.160
and understand how to make
sense of the data that

00:30:28.160 --> 00:30:29.350
were stored there.

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:30.690
And the community
said, look, we're

00:30:30.690 --> 00:30:34.460
not waiting for some
publisher, or some sponsor,

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:36.280
to force this on us.

00:30:36.280 --> 00:30:37.620
We want to get
together and come up

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:41.179
with a minimal set of
information components

00:30:41.179 --> 00:30:43.200
that need to be
described in order for us

00:30:43.200 --> 00:30:46.970
to understand what someone else
may have done with the gene

00:30:46.970 --> 00:30:47.970
microarray.

00:30:47.970 --> 00:30:49.630
And that means,
basically, understanding

00:30:49.630 --> 00:30:53.090
what the raw data are, how
the processing was done,

00:30:53.090 --> 00:30:56.200
how the sample was
annotated, and all the kinds

00:30:56.200 --> 00:30:58.450
of features that are
critical in order to make

00:30:58.450 --> 00:31:00.960
sense of these kinds of data.

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:03.560
And what's really very
exciting is not only

00:31:03.560 --> 00:31:08.520
has the microarray community
created this kind of standard,

00:31:08.520 --> 00:31:09.820
but lots of other groups have.

00:31:09.820 --> 00:31:14.190
If you go to biosharing.org,
which Susanna Sansone will

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:17.500
be talking about
on October 21st,

00:31:17.500 --> 00:31:20.260
you can find the MIAMI
description online,

00:31:20.260 --> 00:31:23.520
and a way of getting a good
reference for what MIAMI looks

00:31:23.520 --> 00:31:24.960
like, and knowing
how to use MIAMI

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:26.700
to describe microinformation.

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:29.900
But what's great,
biosharing has information

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:34.500
on hundreds of different kinds
of biomedical experiments.

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:36.480
And so going to
biosharing will allow

00:31:36.480 --> 00:31:40.010
you to find out information
about how to represent metadata

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:43.650
in a whole variety of
community-blessed approaches.

00:31:43.650 --> 00:31:45.420
And it's really very
exciting that, I think,

00:31:45.420 --> 00:31:47.539
the biomedical community
is getting together

00:31:47.539 --> 00:31:52.200
and articulating these
kinds of frameworks.

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:54.770
And you'll hear a lot
more about this in October

00:31:54.770 --> 00:31:58.220
when Susanna gives her talk.

00:31:58.220 --> 00:32:00.670
And just to give a little
bit of plug to my own work,

00:32:00.670 --> 00:32:02.440
I should say in
the Cedar project

00:32:02.440 --> 00:32:04.540
that Phil mentioned,
what we're doing in Cedar

00:32:04.540 --> 00:32:07.370
is developing
computational methods

00:32:07.370 --> 00:32:11.330
for taking these kinds of
standardized metadata templates

00:32:11.330 --> 00:32:14.640
that are already being
promulgated by the community,

00:32:14.640 --> 00:32:16.630
and putting them in a
computational form that

00:32:16.630 --> 00:32:19.890
makes it really easy, we hope,
for investors to fill out

00:32:19.890 --> 00:32:22.549
these checklists and
templates, and to create

00:32:22.549 --> 00:32:26.730
the kinds of robust and
complete metadata that we think

00:32:26.730 --> 00:32:30.600
are going to be really, really
important in order to ensure

00:32:30.600 --> 00:32:34.950
that the information that is
stored in public repository

00:32:34.950 --> 00:32:38.620
provides enough description so
that people can find what they

00:32:38.620 --> 00:32:41.160
need and put it to use.

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:46.309
Now, you'll notice when we look
at metadata templates like this

00:32:46.309 --> 00:32:48.409
cell-like template
from the Links project

00:32:48.409 --> 00:32:51.020
that's in Cedar, there are lots
of blanks to get filled in.

00:32:51.020 --> 00:32:53.880
And one nominally could
fill out these blanks

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:56.620
just by typing in
text of any kind.

00:32:56.620 --> 00:32:57.870
But one of the
things that, I think,

00:32:57.870 --> 00:33:00.470
is really important
for creating metadata,

00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:02.779
and something that the data
science committee recognizes

00:33:02.779 --> 00:33:05.330
increasingly, is that
what we really want to do

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:07.210
is fill in those
blanks with terms

00:33:07.210 --> 00:33:10.000
from standardized
terminologies or ontologies.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:12.300
I don't have time
this morning to talk

00:33:12.300 --> 00:33:14.160
about ontologies in any detail.

00:33:14.160 --> 00:33:17.360
I know that on October
7, Michel Dumontier

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:19.760
is going to give a whole
talk on ontologies.

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:21.900
This slide, we showed
you a little snippet

00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:24.230
from the National Cancer
Institute Thesaurus

00:33:24.230 --> 00:33:27.210
is one ontology that's
become very important

00:33:27.210 --> 00:33:28.210
for cancer biology.

00:33:28.210 --> 00:33:30.309
And all these
hundreds of ontologies

00:33:30.309 --> 00:33:31.610
that are available
in biomedicine

00:33:31.610 --> 00:33:34.320
provide controlled
terms that make

00:33:34.320 --> 00:33:38.059
it possible for investigators to
fill in blanks using terms that

00:33:38.059 --> 00:33:41.490
are precise, that have
agreed upon meanings,

00:33:41.490 --> 00:33:44.070
and basically will be
recognized for those meanings

00:33:44.070 --> 00:33:47.320
when people process the kinds
of metadata descriptions

00:33:47.320 --> 00:33:53.919
that we need in order to
access metadata online.

00:33:53.919 --> 00:33:55.700
So we've planned
out data management.

00:33:55.700 --> 00:33:57.320
We've collected our data.

00:33:57.320 --> 00:33:58.929
We've performed
quality assurance.

00:33:58.929 --> 00:34:03.250
We've described our data using
templates and ontologies.

00:34:03.250 --> 00:34:04.830
Now, we have to
preserve the data.

00:34:04.830 --> 00:34:07.289
We have to put the data on
our own server, so to speak,

00:34:07.289 --> 00:34:10.179
in order to be able to use
those data for our own analyses.

00:34:10.179 --> 00:34:13.580
And, ultimately, we want to
be able to preserve those data

00:34:13.580 --> 00:34:17.580
for the future so that
others can access our data,

00:34:17.580 --> 00:34:19.830
make sense of those data, and
do the kinds of experiments

00:34:19.830 --> 00:34:21.380
that Khatri does.

00:34:21.380 --> 00:34:26.730
You would think putting data
on a disk is not all that hard.

00:34:26.730 --> 00:34:30.750
You would think that knowing
how to put data in a form

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:33.940
where people could access it
in a few days, in a few months,

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:35.820
or a few years is
not that difficult.

00:34:35.820 --> 00:34:38.820
But, actually, it
requires a lot of planning

00:34:38.820 --> 00:34:41.030
and a lot of thought.

00:34:41.030 --> 00:34:43.290
And perhaps we go back
to the NASA example,

00:34:43.290 --> 00:34:45.200
since it sometimes is
fun to pick on NASA.

00:34:45.200 --> 00:34:46.830
But, certainly,
as someone who is

00:34:46.830 --> 00:34:49.389
very captivated myself
by the space program,

00:34:49.389 --> 00:34:53.070
and got so excited by what
NASA was doing in the 1960s

00:34:53.070 --> 00:34:56.609
and 1970s, one of the
more famous stories

00:34:56.609 --> 00:34:59.210
to come out of that
era concerns the fact

00:34:59.210 --> 00:35:02.050
that much of what came
out of the Apollo program

00:35:02.050 --> 00:35:05.530
was captured in a
variety of data formats,

00:35:05.530 --> 00:35:09.690
ranging from numerical
data sets to video.

00:35:09.690 --> 00:35:12.410
Probably the video that
we all remember the most

00:35:12.410 --> 00:35:15.290
is the video of Neil
Armstrong when he first

00:35:15.290 --> 00:35:17.520
set foot on the moon.

00:35:17.520 --> 00:35:21.720
Many of us watched these videos
with just absolute amazement

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:25.800
to think that people were
standing on the lunar surface.

00:35:25.800 --> 00:35:31.620
What was interesting is that
around 2006, there was suddenly

00:35:31.620 --> 00:35:36.770
a recognition that when you look
at the still photos of people

00:35:36.770 --> 00:35:39.470
in Mission Control watching
the astronauts on the lunar

00:35:39.470 --> 00:35:42.980
surface, at least in
the Apollo 11 mission,

00:35:42.980 --> 00:35:44.770
the videos weren't
nearly this grainy

00:35:44.770 --> 00:35:46.630
and weren't nearly this bad.

00:35:46.630 --> 00:35:50.099
And, in fact, when you
look at the stills,

00:35:50.099 --> 00:35:53.750
you see that in real
time, mission control

00:35:53.750 --> 00:35:56.050
was seeing phenomenal
photos that

00:35:56.050 --> 00:35:59.520
were taken from the video
available from the lunar

00:35:59.520 --> 00:36:01.530
surface, whereas those
of us watching on TV

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:04.770
saw these kinds
of blurry images.

00:36:04.770 --> 00:36:09.589
And there was a great revelation
that took place in Australia,

00:36:09.589 --> 00:36:11.160
where the original
signals from the moon

00:36:11.160 --> 00:36:13.730
were collected in the
Apollo 11 mission.

00:36:13.730 --> 00:36:16.780
And this article, which appeared
in the Sydney Morning Herald

00:36:16.780 --> 00:36:20.300
on October 7, 2006
describes what

00:36:20.300 --> 00:36:24.310
was presented as one
giant blunder for mankind,

00:36:24.310 --> 00:36:27.120
because, basically,
what happened in 1969

00:36:27.120 --> 00:36:31.990
was that the really high
density video image that

00:36:31.990 --> 00:36:34.970
came from the lunar
surface was stored

00:36:34.970 --> 00:36:39.550
in a particular format that
got saved on magnetic tape.

00:36:39.550 --> 00:36:43.750
What the rest of us saw, because
that particular format was not

00:36:43.750 --> 00:36:46.480
something that could be
broadcast internationally,

00:36:46.480 --> 00:36:50.240
was a monitor that was
displaying the high quality

00:36:50.240 --> 00:36:53.300
image coming from the
moon that was being filmed

00:36:53.300 --> 00:36:56.119
by a standard video camera
so that the signal could

00:36:56.119 --> 00:36:59.770
be sent out in NTSC formats
to the rest of the broadcast

00:36:59.770 --> 00:37:00.770
world.

00:37:00.770 --> 00:37:05.250
And it is actually
a video of that NTSC

00:37:05.250 --> 00:37:06.480
that we have preserved.

00:37:06.480 --> 00:37:08.869
And the original
high quality video

00:37:08.869 --> 00:37:11.369
of Neil Armstrong
stepping on to the moon

00:37:11.369 --> 00:37:15.030
was stored on a videotape.

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:18.580
That sounds great until you
hear that sometime in the 1970s,

00:37:18.580 --> 00:37:21.220
NASA realized that it
needed some more video tape,

00:37:21.220 --> 00:37:23.320
and recorded over the
original videotape that

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:25.900
had the high quality
transmission stored on it.

00:37:25.900 --> 00:37:29.640
And it's sort of
a really sad story

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:32.640
of failing to have a
data management plan,

00:37:32.640 --> 00:37:35.280
failing to recognize that
this video might have

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:41.181
lasting value to humanity,
and that an expedient need

00:37:41.181 --> 00:37:45.000
to have a new tape to
copy some new data on lead

00:37:45.000 --> 00:37:47.480
to the loss of something which
I think most people would view

00:37:47.480 --> 00:37:50.320
as extremely important.

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:52.630
And so it's sort of a
way of making us all

00:37:52.630 --> 00:37:56.860
remember that
media are critical,

00:37:56.860 --> 00:38:00.450
that the media that we use
today to store our data

00:38:00.450 --> 00:38:04.570
may not be the media that
we're using in 10 or 15 years.

00:38:04.570 --> 00:38:07.380
I think all of us who've been
around long enough, thinking

00:38:07.380 --> 00:38:11.980
about what kind of data storage
we had in the 1970s and 1980s,

00:38:11.980 --> 00:38:13.510
that's pretty clear.

00:38:13.510 --> 00:38:15.839
And there's also the issue of
just what people have called

00:38:15.839 --> 00:38:20.109
bit rot-- that over
time, sometimes

00:38:20.109 --> 00:38:22.109
the way in which we
store data even digitally

00:38:22.109 --> 00:38:23.460
leads to loss of information.

00:38:23.460 --> 00:38:26.470
And we have to recognize
that we need copies.

00:38:26.470 --> 00:38:27.810
And we need backups.

00:38:27.810 --> 00:38:31.610
And the good news is on
September 30th Pascal Gaudet

00:38:31.610 --> 00:38:33.180
is going to be talking
about data curation.

00:38:33.180 --> 00:38:36.640
And [s]he'll talk a lot more,
some of the physical problems

00:38:36.640 --> 00:38:38.930
of storing data, and
how to avoid some

00:38:38.930 --> 00:38:44.020
of these really ugly things.

00:38:44.020 --> 00:38:46.200
So life cycle involves planning,
collection, QA, description,

00:38:46.200 --> 00:38:47.200
preservation.

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:51.180
And then, ultimately,
our goal is to discover.

00:38:51.180 --> 00:38:54.450
We want to be able to find
the data that we need--

00:38:54.450 --> 00:38:58.130
not only for our own
experiments, but also,

00:38:58.130 --> 00:39:00.859
as we've discussed already,
for other investigators

00:39:00.859 --> 00:39:03.500
to find our data, and
discover new kinds of things

00:39:03.500 --> 00:39:05.000
from those data.

00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:06.839
And so the good news
is, lots of work

00:39:06.839 --> 00:39:08.990
is taking place in the
biomedical community

00:39:08.990 --> 00:39:09.990
in this area.

00:39:09.990 --> 00:39:13.340
We are no longer stuck in
the era of card catalogs.

00:39:13.340 --> 00:39:17.850
We have all kinds of technology,
like the DataMed system coming

00:39:17.850 --> 00:39:20.020
out of the bioCADDIE community.

00:39:20.020 --> 00:39:23.740
And so the good news is in this
seminar series on September 16,

00:39:23.740 --> 00:39:25.660
Bill Hirsch will talk
about general problems

00:39:25.660 --> 00:39:27.760
of information retrieval.

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:30.839
And on September 23rd,
Lucila Ohno-Machado from UCSD,

00:39:30.839 --> 00:39:34.420
I'm sure will talk about
DataMed, the tool coming out

00:39:34.420 --> 00:39:37.520
of bioCADDIE, which
allows us to access data

00:39:37.520 --> 00:39:40.560
in a variety of repositories
in a generic way,

00:39:40.560 --> 00:39:44.210
and to search for data so that
we can access the data we need,

00:39:44.210 --> 00:39:48.000
and put data together
in ways which, I think,

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:52.680
will lead to new kinds
of experimental insights.

00:39:52.680 --> 00:39:55.410
And so the data life cycle
continues to circulate.

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:57.300
We want to plan.

00:39:57.300 --> 00:39:58.300
We want to collect.

00:39:58.300 --> 00:40:01.140
We want to describe, and
preserve, and discover,

00:40:01.140 --> 00:40:02.140
and integrate.

00:40:02.140 --> 00:40:03.140
And, frankly, we're
in a stage where

00:40:03.140 --> 00:40:06.680
we are recognizing that this
life cycle just circulates

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:08.570
over, and over, and over again.

00:40:08.570 --> 00:40:11.410
As data become
available online, and we

00:40:11.410 --> 00:40:13.250
can experiment with
this data-- and, again,

00:40:13.250 --> 00:40:14.960
without even picking
up a pipette,

00:40:14.960 --> 00:40:19.000
we can learn new things
about biomedicine.

00:40:19.000 --> 00:40:22.990
And I think the buzzword that
is now permeating the community

00:40:22.990 --> 00:40:26.280
that you'll hear a lot
about in upcoming talks

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:30.460
is this notion of making data
fair-- the idea that data

00:40:30.460 --> 00:40:34.119
should be findable, that
it should be accessible,

00:40:34.119 --> 00:40:35.500
interoperable, and reusable.

00:40:35.500 --> 00:40:38.170
These terms came
out of a workshop

00:40:38.170 --> 00:40:41.090
that took place in the
Netherlands a couple years ago.

00:40:41.090 --> 00:40:43.600
And now they're being adopted
widely in the Elixir

00:40:43.600 --> 00:40:47.380
community in Europe, the BD2K
community in the United States.

00:40:47.380 --> 00:40:49.921
And this notion of
fair data, where

00:40:49.921 --> 00:40:52.680
it's possible to find data
that may have been created

00:40:52.680 --> 00:40:55.210
by other investigators,
where the data are

00:40:55.210 --> 00:40:57.380
accessible because
they're stored in formats

00:40:57.380 --> 00:40:59.230
that we understand,
that we can parse,

00:40:59.230 --> 00:41:01.470
and that we can
interpret, that they're

00:41:01.470 --> 00:41:04.260
interoperable because we can
relate our data to the data

00:41:04.260 --> 00:41:07.339
that we have available
online, and make sense

00:41:07.339 --> 00:41:08.930
of a variety of data sets.

00:41:08.930 --> 00:41:13.470
And we use those data to create
new insights, to identify

00:41:13.470 --> 00:41:16.310
as Khatri did, a genomic
signature for sepsis,

00:41:16.310 --> 00:41:18.290
or a genomic signature
or active TB.

00:41:18.290 --> 00:41:20.840
These are the kinds of
experiments and the kinds

00:41:20.840 --> 00:41:23.000
of insights that are going
to come from the data

00:41:23.000 --> 00:41:26.190
that we have online, and
frankly are going to increase

00:41:26.190 --> 00:41:28.330
the effectiveness of the
biomedical enterprise

00:41:28.330 --> 00:41:31.400
by orders of magnitude,
because not only will we

00:41:31.400 --> 00:41:34.410
have the insights that come
from the initial experiments.

00:41:34.410 --> 00:41:37.850
But we're going to be able to
have tons of investigators who

00:41:37.850 --> 00:41:40.210
will be looking at
existing data sets

00:41:40.210 --> 00:41:43.510
and coming up with insights that
were just impossible to make

00:41:43.510 --> 00:41:46.339
at the time that the data
were originally collected,

00:41:46.339 --> 00:41:49.000
either because the original
investigators never thought

00:41:49.000 --> 00:41:51.950
of it, or because in
retrospect we understand more

00:41:51.950 --> 00:41:55.700
about the world than we may
have understood previously,

00:41:55.700 --> 00:41:59.420
and allow us to think
about new kinds of analyses

00:41:59.420 --> 00:42:03.040
we can perform, and new
kinds of investigations.

00:42:03.040 --> 00:42:06.140
That's why now,
40, 50 years later,

00:42:06.140 --> 00:42:08.619
NASA is working really
hard to figure out

00:42:08.619 --> 00:42:12.290
how it can try to recover
many of the datasets that

00:42:12.290 --> 00:42:14.850
were created during
the 1960s, and stored

00:42:14.850 --> 00:42:17.099
on media that can
no longer be read

00:42:17.099 --> 00:42:20.130
because the kinds of machines
that read them no longer exist,

00:42:20.130 --> 00:42:22.540
because now that we understand
all that we've learned

00:42:22.540 --> 00:42:25.369
over the past 40 years, we
want to go back to those data

00:42:25.369 --> 00:42:26.369
and reinterpret them.

00:42:26.369 --> 00:42:28.690
And that's exactly what's going
to be happening in biomedicine,

00:42:28.690 --> 00:42:31.560
as we try to go to
old datasets that

00:42:31.560 --> 00:42:32.990
are available in
the repositories

00:42:32.990 --> 00:42:35.859
that we manage throughout
the biomedical enterprise,

00:42:35.859 --> 00:42:38.030
and start interpreting
those data anew,

00:42:38.030 --> 00:42:41.470
in terms of all the things that
we'll learn in the interim.

00:42:41.470 --> 00:42:43.490
That's the very exciting part.

00:42:43.490 --> 00:42:45.790
And that's one of the
reasons that having fair data

00:42:45.790 --> 00:42:47.080
is so important to us.

00:42:47.080 --> 00:42:49.960
There's also, if you will,
a dark side that makes

00:42:49.960 --> 00:42:51.670
fair data just as important.

00:42:51.670 --> 00:42:54.150
And that is the
increasing mistrust

00:42:54.150 --> 00:42:56.809
that people have had in science.

00:42:56.809 --> 00:42:59.320
People may remember the
famous Economist front page

00:42:59.320 --> 00:43:02.780
article that occurred about two
or three years ago, discussing

00:43:02.780 --> 00:43:05.200
the problems that
scientists have

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:27.470
in being able to reproduce--
Was that a question?

00:43:27.470 --> 00:43:31.970
OK, relating how Amgen tried
really hard to reproduce

00:43:31.970 --> 00:43:35.810
the findings in 63 landmark
papers in cancer biology,

00:43:35.810 --> 00:43:39.441
and claim that they only
do so in about six of them.

00:43:39.441 --> 00:43:43.120
When Bayer tried to reproduce
67 preclinical studies, and said

00:43:43.120 --> 00:43:44.780
they were successful in
validating the results

00:43:44.780 --> 00:43:48.800
in only 25%, the
notion that many

00:43:48.800 --> 00:43:52.430
of these landmark studies
that ultimately may have been

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:56.000
difficult to reproduce spawn
entire fields of investigation,

00:43:56.000 --> 00:43:58.970
with no one bothering to
confirm the initial results,

00:43:58.970 --> 00:44:00.829
but taking them for
granted, and trying

00:44:00.829 --> 00:44:04.010
to do new experiments that,
basically and allegedly would

00:44:04.010 --> 00:44:05.900
build on those
irreproducible results.

00:44:05.900 --> 00:44:09.609
And, frankly, as
many of us know,

00:44:09.609 --> 00:44:12.160
it is the non-reproducible
studies that

00:44:12.160 --> 00:44:14.390
are more likely to get
published in the journals

00:44:14.390 --> 00:44:15.950
and have the highest
impact factors,

00:44:15.950 --> 00:44:18.800
because often those
non-reproducible studies,

00:44:18.800 --> 00:44:21.600
frankly, have the
most exciting results,

00:44:21.600 --> 00:44:24.579
even though it may be often
difficult in some cases

00:44:24.579 --> 00:44:25.579
to reproduce.

00:44:25.579 --> 00:44:27.670
And, of course, this
is a problem that's

00:44:27.670 --> 00:44:28.820
gotten a lot of hand-wringing.

00:44:28.820 --> 00:44:30.031
Lots of people talk about it.

00:44:30.031 --> 00:44:33.349
It has lots of causes.

00:44:33.349 --> 00:44:34.460
And some of them
are pretty trivial--

00:44:34.460 --> 00:44:37.660
sometimes just not enough
statistical power in the study.

00:44:37.660 --> 00:44:40.790
Sometimes, there's an art form
in conducting the experiments.

00:44:40.790 --> 00:44:42.450
And the metadata
themselves don't

00:44:42.450 --> 00:44:44.550
capture all that information.

00:44:44.550 --> 00:44:49.349
There's often an eagerness to
publish earlier, or frankly

00:44:49.349 --> 00:44:50.349
prematurely.

00:44:50.349 --> 00:44:52.030
Rarely, there's fraud.

00:44:52.030 --> 00:44:54.839
But, really, I think
for many of us,

00:44:54.839 --> 00:44:58.200
we have a problem where we have
a system which traditionally

00:44:58.200 --> 00:45:00.579
has not made it
easy or rewarding

00:45:00.579 --> 00:45:02.680
for investigators to
replicate the results

00:45:02.680 --> 00:45:04.720
of other researchers.

00:45:04.720 --> 00:45:08.200
And we have a culture that
is only now recognizing

00:45:08.200 --> 00:45:11.190
the importance of data
preservation, description,

00:45:11.190 --> 00:45:12.960
and re-exploration.

00:45:12.960 --> 00:45:16.990
But as we pay more attention
to the data lifecycle,

00:45:16.990 --> 00:45:21.920
as we think about how we
can use other people's data,

00:45:21.920 --> 00:45:23.839
how we can learn from
other people's data,

00:45:23.839 --> 00:45:26.420
why it's important to
re-validate other people's

00:45:26.420 --> 00:45:31.349
data, I honestly believe that
the world is going to change.

00:45:31.349 --> 00:45:33.849
And at a minimum, we're
going to see science moving

00:45:33.849 --> 00:45:37.859
to a situation where we have
even more open, online access

00:45:37.859 --> 00:45:40.839
to experimental
datasets, where there's

00:45:40.839 --> 00:45:44.200
an emphasis on annotation
of online datasets

00:45:44.200 --> 00:45:47.560
with adequate metadata,
that not only will we

00:45:47.560 --> 00:45:49.740
try to have the minimal
information about data

00:45:49.740 --> 00:45:50.830
available online.

00:45:50.830 --> 00:45:53.589
But I think,
ultimately, we're going

00:45:53.589 --> 00:45:56.670
to see so much high
quality metadata online

00:45:56.670 --> 00:46:03.030
that the online data may well
be just as useful as a journal

00:46:03.030 --> 00:46:06.550
publication in understanding
what scientists have done,

00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:09.840
and how to make
sense of their work.

00:46:09.840 --> 00:46:12.470
There's increasing emphasis
on the use of ontology

00:46:12.470 --> 00:46:16.579
to be able to annotate
metadata in a reproducible way.

00:46:16.579 --> 00:46:20.250
And, ultimately, as
systems like DataMed

00:46:20.250 --> 00:46:24.329
lead to better search
for experimental results,

00:46:24.329 --> 00:46:26.960
we're going to be able to
find the data that we need,

00:46:26.960 --> 00:46:28.369
and analyze them better.

00:46:28.369 --> 00:46:31.280
Fundamentally, what
we're moving toward

00:46:31.280 --> 00:46:32.900
is a world where
there's much more

00:46:32.900 --> 00:46:34.640
concern about data stewardship.

00:46:34.640 --> 00:46:39.980
And we recognize that
the life cycle is really

00:46:39.980 --> 00:46:41.730
where we need to be
focusing our attention.

00:46:41.730 --> 00:46:45.670
So, ultimately, we're
thinking about a revolution

00:46:45.670 --> 00:46:48.810
in the way we talk about
science and we talk about data.

00:46:48.810 --> 00:46:51.160
We're going to be
thinking about a world

00:46:51.160 --> 00:46:52.849
where, invariably,
data are going

00:46:52.849 --> 00:46:56.000
to be coming at us with
increasing volume, velocity,

00:46:56.000 --> 00:46:57.100
and variety.

00:46:57.100 --> 00:47:00.850
And as a consequence, the kinds
of experiments that we do,

00:47:00.850 --> 00:47:02.829
and the way that
we manage our data,

00:47:02.829 --> 00:47:05.570
are going to require
lots of new computational

00:47:05.570 --> 00:47:07.740
approaches-- new approaches
that people are working on

00:47:07.740 --> 00:47:10.850
right now, and that you'll learn
about in this seminar series.

00:47:10.850 --> 00:47:16.000
We're recognizing that, because
data need to be validated,

00:47:16.000 --> 00:47:19.550
and re-interpreted, and
re-explored, investigators will

00:47:19.550 --> 00:47:22.580
no longer think of data
as the side effect,

00:47:22.580 --> 00:47:25.970
or the end product, that
allows them to come up

00:47:25.970 --> 00:47:27.790
with the real result
of the research

00:47:27.790 --> 00:47:30.290
with a scientific paper,
but rather to treat

00:47:30.290 --> 00:47:34.110
the data as the actual
purpose of the work,

00:47:34.110 --> 00:47:36.250
and the data as
the end point that

00:47:36.250 --> 00:47:38.600
is complemented by
the scientific paper,

00:47:38.600 --> 00:47:40.480
but that also is
going to provide

00:47:40.480 --> 00:47:42.970
the basis for future
experimentation

00:47:42.970 --> 00:47:46.730
by a whole cadre of
investigators, who may not even

00:47:46.730 --> 00:47:50.329
have been born yet, and with
the recognition that data should

00:47:50.329 --> 00:47:55.200
be fair, findable, accessible,
interoperable, and reusable.

00:47:55.200 --> 00:47:56.829
Investigators will
begin to need to plan

00:47:56.829 --> 00:48:00.130
for data management in much
more comprehensive ways

00:48:00.130 --> 00:48:02.369
before they even begin
their experiments,

00:48:02.369 --> 00:48:05.420
during their experiments
as conditions change,

00:48:05.420 --> 00:48:07.130
and even after
their experiments,

00:48:07.130 --> 00:48:09.150
as they recognize that
there may be better ways

00:48:09.150 --> 00:48:11.550
to make the data more
publicly available so

00:48:11.550 --> 00:48:14.490
that other investigators
can take advantage

00:48:14.490 --> 00:48:17.140
of the kinds of data
that are being produced

00:48:17.140 --> 00:48:19.890
in the biomedical enterprise.

00:48:19.890 --> 00:48:22.390
So there's a life cycle
that I've mentioned.

00:48:22.390 --> 00:48:24.040
And if you think
about the lifecycle,

00:48:24.040 --> 00:48:26.630
not only does this
provide a good framework

00:48:26.630 --> 00:48:29.060
for thinking about how
data are collected,

00:48:29.060 --> 00:48:32.310
and managed, and
used, and discovered,

00:48:32.310 --> 00:48:35.780
and used to form the basis
of new experimentation.

00:48:35.780 --> 00:48:37.670
But, basically, what
I think captures

00:48:37.670 --> 00:48:39.970
all of the main high
points of data science

00:48:39.970 --> 00:48:42.790
that you'll hear about
in this lecture series.

00:48:42.790 --> 00:48:45.119
And so the most important
thing I can leave you with

00:48:45.119 --> 00:48:48.040
is that coming up are going to
be some really fantastic talks

00:48:48.040 --> 00:48:49.440
by some really great people.

00:48:49.440 --> 00:48:51.550
And I hope that
what I've said today

00:48:51.550 --> 00:48:53.780
will inspire you to
tune in every week

00:48:53.780 --> 00:48:57.490
and hear about what's going
on on the cutting edge of data

00:48:57.490 --> 00:48:59.690
science and biomedicine.

00:48:59.690 --> 00:49:01.310
And let me leave you
with a couple references

00:49:01.310 --> 00:49:04.310
that I think I found really
useful in thinking about some

00:49:04.310 --> 00:49:05.680
of the things I've talked
about this morning,

00:49:05.680 --> 00:49:07.570
and hopefully
you'll find valuable

00:49:07.570 --> 00:49:08.849
as you think about
how you're going

00:49:08.849 --> 00:49:11.780
to be taking advantage of
data management planning,

00:49:11.780 --> 00:49:13.119
thinking about the
data lifecycle,

00:49:13.119 --> 00:49:15.000
and putting it to
use in your own work.

00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:16.450
Let me stop there.

00:49:16.450 --> 00:49:23.960
And see if we have some time for
some questions, perhaps.

00:49:23.960 --> 00:49:28.829
Michelle Dunn: Thank you, Mark.

00:49:28.829 --> 00:49:31.760
This is wonderful.

00:49:31.760 --> 00:49:36.109
You gave a great overview of
what we will see coming up.

00:49:36.109 --> 00:49:41.450
And I know I, for one, am
really looking forward to this.

00:49:41.450 --> 00:49:44.220
I think if there
are any questions,

00:49:44.220 --> 00:49:46.800
we need to have
them by the chat,

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:51.480
because everyone logged
on is in silent mode.

00:49:51.480 --> 00:49:55.050
So if you do have a
question, please just

00:49:55.050 --> 00:49:59.700
write it in the chat box on the
right hand side of the Control

00:49:59.700 --> 00:50:03.220
panel, or on your control
panel, wherever it is.

00:50:03.220 --> 00:50:18.740
And Dr. Musen might have time
to take a couple of questions.

00:50:18.740 --> 00:50:31.130
So there was one that came up
earlier under the question box.

00:50:31.130 --> 00:50:35.410
And it's about fair data
and interoperability.

00:50:35.410 --> 00:50:40.150
And it says, given the
challenges with legacy data

00:50:40.150 --> 00:50:43.540
interoperability-- for example,
electronic health record

00:50:43.540 --> 00:50:46.210
limitations, as far
as exchanging data--

00:50:46.210 --> 00:50:48.450
will this lecture or
future lecture discuss,

00:50:48.450 --> 00:50:51.550
or can you discuss,
how to reconcile

00:50:51.550 --> 00:50:58.550
the need for open fair data, and
the underlying interoperability

00:50:58.550 --> 00:51:00.520
challenges?

00:51:00.520 --> 00:51:10.390
Mark, are you still there?

00:51:10.390 --> 00:51:20.480
Mark Musen: Did I get muted?

00:51:20.480 --> 00:51:26.510
Michelle Dunn: I think you did get
muted for a bit.

00:51:26.510 --> 00:51:28.380
Mark Musen: Am I back?

00:51:28.380 --> 00:51:30.210
Michelle Dunn: You are back now, though, Mark.

00:51:30.210 --> 00:51:34.910
Mark Musen: OK, I won't take it personally.

00:51:34.910 --> 00:51:37.490
That was a great question.

00:51:37.490 --> 00:51:39.500
And it's more than I can
answer in a couple minutes.

00:51:39.500 --> 00:51:41.319
But I think there are
problems that you point out,

00:51:41.319 --> 00:51:45.420
because the way that we do
experiments change over time.

00:51:45.420 --> 00:51:48.870
Sometimes, we talk about
microdata being re-examined.

00:51:48.870 --> 00:51:50.569
Obviously, the kinds
of chips that people

00:51:50.569 --> 00:51:53.700
use change over time, and being
able to go back and make sense

00:51:53.700 --> 00:51:55.230
of some of the very
oldest technology

00:51:55.230 --> 00:51:57.520
is always challenging.

00:51:57.520 --> 00:52:00.210
A lot of the challenges
occur because the way we

00:52:00.210 --> 00:52:01.280
think about the world changes.

00:52:01.280 --> 00:52:04.599
So I mentioned ontologies
as being an important way

00:52:04.599 --> 00:52:09.730
to allow for interoperability
among heterogeneous data sets.

00:52:09.730 --> 00:52:12.869
But our ontologies
themselves change over time.

00:52:12.869 --> 00:52:14.500
We know, for example,
that data that

00:52:14.500 --> 00:52:17.780
were analyzed with old
versions of the gene ontology

00:52:17.780 --> 00:52:19.960
will give results that
are different from data

00:52:19.960 --> 00:52:22.910
that are analyzed with new
versions of the gene ontology.

00:52:22.910 --> 00:52:25.280
And so I don't
want to say that we

00:52:25.280 --> 00:52:30.119
have a panacea for dealing
with change over time

00:52:30.119 --> 00:52:32.730
in these experimental data sets.

00:52:32.730 --> 00:52:36.020
But we do have real opportunity
for really exciting research

00:52:36.020 --> 00:52:39.781
in how to reconcile ontologies
that change over time, how

00:52:39.781 --> 00:52:42.770
to ensure that data have
sufficient annotation that we

00:52:42.770 --> 00:52:45.750
can back and reconstruct what
are all the assumptions that

00:52:45.750 --> 00:52:47.810
may be relevant in trying
to integrate data that

00:52:47.810 --> 00:52:50.560
were created at
different points in time

00:52:50.560 --> 00:52:54.500
under different circumstances,
and recognizing that it's not

00:52:54.500 --> 00:52:57.420
just a matter of mixing
and matching data sets.

00:52:57.420 --> 00:52:59.690
But there really is
a science to being

00:52:59.690 --> 00:53:03.360
able to innumerate what are
the assumptions behind data,

00:53:03.360 --> 00:53:06.480
and to be able to reconcile
those assumptions as data

00:53:06.480 --> 00:53:08.369
get integrated.

00:53:08.369 --> 00:53:11.210
Michelle Dunn: Thanks.

00:53:11.210 --> 00:53:12.700
There are quite a
few other questions

00:53:12.700 --> 00:53:17.500
under the question box.

00:53:17.500 --> 00:53:19.480
One of them that I
see on the very bottom

00:53:19.480 --> 00:53:22.400
is, do you think
that this data life

00:53:22.400 --> 00:53:24.220
cycle applies to
all kinds of data,

00:53:24.220 --> 00:53:26.050
other than biomedical data?

00:53:26.050 --> 00:53:31.730
Mark Musen: Yes, and I can say that the
first reference that you

00:53:31.730 --> 00:53:34.410
see on the screen now is one
from the DataOne community,

00:53:34.410 --> 00:53:37.280
which is concerned
with earth sciences.

00:53:37.280 --> 00:53:40.510
And I think they represent a
different kind of community,

00:53:40.510 --> 00:53:43.080
but have exactly the
same kinds of data needs,

00:53:43.080 --> 00:53:44.990
and recognize exactly
the same problems.

00:53:44.990 --> 00:53:47.932
I think the issues we're talking
about relate to all of science.

00:53:47.932 --> 00:53:51.480
And I think they
also relate to data

00:53:51.480 --> 00:53:53.710
that are not necessarily
created through

00:53:53.710 --> 00:53:55.380
controlled experimentation.

00:53:55.380 --> 00:53:58.099
I think they relate to
observational studies,

00:53:58.099 --> 00:54:00.040
as much as they do
interventional studies.

00:54:00.040 --> 00:54:03.210
I think they relate
to signal data that

00:54:03.210 --> 00:54:04.280
are acquired opportunistically.

00:54:04.280 --> 00:54:07.000
And I think to a
certain degree, they

00:54:07.000 --> 00:54:08.570
relate to the kind of
data we get from EHRs.

00:54:08.570 --> 00:54:12.890
So I think, in a sense, all
of these issues are relevant.

00:54:12.890 --> 00:54:15.950
And I think we're going to be
seeing a persistence of data

00:54:15.950 --> 00:54:18.460
in all these forms and
re-exploration of those data

00:54:18.460 --> 00:54:22.070
from all those various
sources in the future.

00:54:22.070 --> 00:54:29.790
Michelle Dunn: OK, I think we have time
for maybe one more question.

00:54:29.790 --> 00:54:34.359
There is one here
about data sharing.

00:54:34.359 --> 00:54:37.180
And it says that there
has been much progress

00:54:37.180 --> 00:54:39.640
in sharing molecular data.

00:54:39.640 --> 00:54:44.040
But there are challenges to
clinical data, particularly

00:54:44.040 --> 00:54:48.910
challenges around privacy
consent and other issues,

00:54:48.910 --> 00:54:52.800
and that investigators are
hesitant to share the data.

00:54:52.800 --> 00:54:56.760
Do you foresee directions for
tackling these challenges?

00:54:56.760 --> 00:55:02.420
Mark Musen: Well, I think one of the biggest
challenges in data sharing

00:55:02.420 --> 00:55:05.700
is the WIIFM problem.

00:55:05.700 --> 00:55:07.200
That's not a radio station.

00:55:07.200 --> 00:55:09.410
It's, what's in it for me?

00:55:09.410 --> 00:55:12.530
And I think investigators need
to be able to understand that

00:55:12.530 --> 00:55:16.470
by making their data public,
not only will they learn more

00:55:16.470 --> 00:55:18.280
because other people
may have insights

00:55:18.280 --> 00:55:20.130
that they can contribute back.

00:55:20.130 --> 00:55:22.910
But also, I think it's part
of the scientific enterprise.

00:55:22.910 --> 00:55:25.960
And I think one of the
things that is changing

00:55:25.960 --> 00:55:29.079
is a recognition that science
doesn't stop with publication

00:55:29.079 --> 00:55:32.550
of a bunch of prose, but with
the opportunity of others

00:55:32.550 --> 00:55:35.540
and the community at large to
be able to look at data sets.

00:55:35.540 --> 00:55:39.980
That said, not only will people,
I hope, act altruistically.

00:55:39.980 --> 00:55:41.880
But, clearly,
funders are thinking

00:55:41.880 --> 00:55:44.940
about doing what they can
to ensure that the data are

00:55:44.940 --> 00:55:45.940
made available.

00:55:45.940 --> 00:55:48.160
And the basic
sentiment is that data

00:55:48.160 --> 00:55:51.619
are collected as a consequence
of public investment

00:55:51.619 --> 00:55:53.380
and research belong
to the public,

00:55:53.380 --> 00:55:55.660
and therefore need
to be made available.

00:55:55.660 --> 00:55:58.810
That is, obviously, going to
be different in the case of EHR

00:55:58.810 --> 00:56:00.609
data, which have
a different value.

00:56:00.609 --> 00:56:03.359
But at the same time,
I think patients

00:56:03.359 --> 00:56:06.500
who recognize that the learning
health system as a model

00:56:06.500 --> 00:56:08.510
is going to be making
medical care better

00:56:08.510 --> 00:56:12.090
and more precise in the future,
also recognize that they may

00:56:12.090 --> 00:56:13.190
want to make their
data available,

00:56:13.190 --> 00:56:16.920
even if their providers may
not, because those data will

00:56:16.920 --> 00:56:20.119
ensure that the care of future
patients may be more refined.

00:56:20.119 --> 00:56:22.500
And so I think a
lot of this depends

00:56:22.500 --> 00:56:26.119
on cultural change, and the
recognition among investigators

00:56:26.119 --> 00:56:28.480
that data sharing is
important, and also

00:56:28.480 --> 00:56:30.150
when it comes to clinical
data, recognition

00:56:30.150 --> 00:56:32.650
on the part of patients
that they can help humanity

00:56:32.650 --> 00:56:35.390
by making their data
available as well.

00:56:35.390 --> 00:56:39.530
Michelle Dunn: Thank you so much, Mark.

00:56:39.530 --> 00:56:45.380
I think if Jack would like
to say the last few words,

00:56:45.380 --> 00:56:51.940
I am not sure they are able to--
I think their audio cut out.

00:56:51.940 --> 00:56:59.329
But unless they want to jump
on, I think we should just say

00:56:59.329 --> 00:57:01.819
thank you to our
speaker, which I

00:57:01.819 --> 00:57:04.619
think everyone is giving
you a silent applause right

00:57:04.619 --> 00:57:05.650
now, Mark.

00:57:05.650 --> 00:57:09.290
But then also, thank you to
everyone who joined then.

00:57:09.290 --> 00:57:14.550
Sorry about the slow
start this morning.

00:57:14.550 --> 00:57:17.559
We will be sending out,
again, the new URL.

00:57:17.559 --> 00:57:21.250
And I particularly want to thank
the training corporation center

00:57:21.250 --> 00:57:24.430
for, at the last
minute, switching over

00:57:24.430 --> 00:57:30.020
to a service that will
accommodate all of us.

00:57:30.020 --> 00:57:31.990
So thank you very much.

00:57:31.990 --> 00:57:36.290
And I hope that you all tune
in in the upcoming weeks,

00:57:36.290 --> 00:57:39.609
because we have a great
program ahead of us.

00:57:39.609 --> 00:57:41.420
Bye now.

